.TH SLIPCASE\-OPEN 1 "2026-08-31" "slipcase\-open 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slipcase\-open \- open the payload of a slipcase container in its own application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slipcase\-open open
.I CONTAINER
.br
.B slipcase\-open sessions
.br
.B slipcase\-open close
.I SESSION
.br
.B slipcase\-open recover
.I SESSION
.RB { \-\-write\-back | \-\-discard }
.br
.B slipcase\-open policy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens the payload of a
.B .slpc
container in whatever application the desktop normally handles that kind of file
with, watches for edits, and writes them back into the container.
.PP
Opening a container starts a
.BR session .
The payload is extracted into a private directory under the user's own state
directory, the platform's trust\-zone mark is carried onto the copy, the
directory is watched, and every save that reaches it is repacked into the
container atomically.
.PP
Sessions are explicit, because no platform reports reliably that an application
has finished with a file. They are listed, they are closed by hand, and one that
survives a crash is offered back rather than written back unasked: the tool was
not watching when the process died, so it cannot tell a complete save from a
half\-written one.
.PP
The first invocation becomes a resident instance and holds the terminal. Every
later invocation hands its container to that one and exits.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI open " CONTAINER"
Start a session and hold it. On a desktop with a notification service, the
instance reports and asks through notifications carrying buttons.
.TP
.B sessions
List what is open on this machine and what was left behind by a session that did
not close.
.TP
.BI close " SESSION"
Write back what the session has and clean it up. Where the application still has
the payload open, the directory is handed to recovery instead of being removed,
so the application's next save has somewhere to land.
.TP
.BI recover " SESSION"
Act on a session left behind. Requires
.B \-\-write\-back
to put the payload into its container, or
.B \-\-discard
to throw it away. Naming a session without either reports its state and does
nothing.
.TP
.B policy
Report where settings are read on this machine, what they resolve to, and where
the tool keeps its own state. The paths below come out of the environment, so
this is what answers which file to edit; the list here is what the defaults are.
Exits non\-zero where a layer exists and cannot be read, after naming it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/slipcase/open.toml
Machine policy. Root\-owned, takes precedence over the user's own configuration,
and installed documenting every key and setting none of them.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/slipcase\-open/policy.toml
The user's own configuration, at
.I ~/.config/slipcase\-open/policy.toml
where the variable is unset. Not created by the package.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/slipcase\-open/sessions
Where a session's payload lives while it is open, at
.I ~/.local/state/slipcase\-open/sessions
where the variable is unset. Deliberately not the temporary directory, which a
reboot or a cleaner may empty underneath an edit that has not been written back.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/slipcase\-open/front\-door
The socket every later invocation reaches the resident instance through. Private
to its owner, and gone at logout. Falls back beside the state directory on a
system that sets no runtime directory.
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/slipcase\-open.desktop
The desktop entry that puts this tool in the Open With list. The media type it
names is declared by
.BR slipcase\-common ,
not by this package, and without that package installed nothing associates a
container with this entry.
.PP
Run
.B slipcase\-open policy
for these paths as this machine resolves them rather than as they are written
here.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The two policy files are TOML and take the same keys. In order of authority:
machine policy, then user policy, then the user's own configuration, then the
built\-in default. There is no per\-user policy file on this platform, because
nothing here would administer one; that layer is Windows and macOS vocabulary.
Every key is optional, and one that is absent means the layer has no opinion
about it rather than that it forbids everything.
.TP
.B allowed
The payload extensions that may be opened, compared with ASCII case folded.
.TP
.B mode
Whether
.B allowed
replaces the built\-in set or adds to it. Must be said out loud; "replace" is the
default.
.TP
.B denied
Extensions refused, whatever every other setting says.
.TP
.B user_may_extend
Whether the user's own configuration is consulted at all.
.TP
.B confirm_each_write_back
Ask before each write\-back rather than repacking as the payload is saved.
.TP
.B notify
How much is said without being asked: "important" by default, or "everything"
for a notification on every write\-back. Nothing here can silence a question.
.SH SECURITY
The allowed set is a guardrail against user error and social engineering in the
convenient path, and not a security boundary. A container is a plain ZIP and any
user can extract its payload with standard tools. The boundary is application
control.
.PP
A payload whose bytes are an executable image under an extension that is not one
is reported and still opened: the extension governs what runs, so refusing would
assert a control this path does not carry. A payload with no usable extension is
refused, because the desktop would otherwise ask which application to run it
with, inside a flow the person reads as opening a document.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR slipcase (1),
.BR slipcase\-desktop (1)
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson
